Title:
CFG-FindHiddenShellcode — Detection of hidden shellcode via CFG bitmap anomalies

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This repository contains a Windows userland tool that detects “hidden” shellcode by analyzing anomalies in Control Flow Guard (CFG) bitmaps. It targets evasion techniques (e.g., Gargoyle-style) that periodically flip memory protections to non-executable to avoid scanners that primarily inspect executable pages. The key observation is that the CFG bitmap is updated when pages become executable, but not cleared when they later become non-executable, leaving historical footprints of previously-executable private memory. By walking the CFG bitmap and correlating it with current page protections/VAD state, the tool flags regions that were executable earlier in the process lifetime but are now NX—strong indicators of protection-fluctuation-based concealment. It’s primarily useful for Blue Team/DFIR and EDR engineers building memory-based detections, and also for Red Teamers validating whether their evasion leaves detectable artifacts. The work is tied to the Black Hat Asia 2023 talk “You Can Run, but You Can't Hide - Finding the Footprints of Hidden Shellcode.”

Technical Focus:
- Windows Control Flow Guard (CFG) bitmap internals and enumeration
- Detection of protection-flipping / executable-to-NX transitions
- Correlation of CFG state with current virtual memory protections (VAD/regions)
- Memory injection and shellcode concealment tradecraft (Gargoyle-like)
- User-mode memory scanning heuristics for CFG-enabled processes

Use Cases:
- Detect shellcode that hides by toggling PAGE_EXECUTE* ↔ PAGE_READWRITE (NX) in live processes
- Build/validate EDR telemetry and heuristics around CFG artifacts
- Triage suspicious processes by identifying private regions with “previously executable” history
- Red team testing of memory-evasion techniques against CFG-based detection ideas

Keywords:
Control Flow Guard, CFG bitmap, Windows internals, memory scanning, shellcode detection, Gargoyle, memory protection flipping, PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE, NX, Virtual Address Descriptor, VAD tree, private memory, process memory forensics, injection detection, Black Hat Asia 2023, EDR detection engineering, user-mode analysis, thread injection, trampolines